ReasonIQ (formerly BiasLess): Real-Time AI-Powered Debate and Critical Thinking Analysis System

Project Report – Comprehensive System Report, Implementation & Live Evaluation


1. Project Overview

BiasLess is an NLP-based system designed to analyze debate speech and provide explainable feedback on claims, evidence, logical fallacies, bias-aware language indicators, sentiment, named entities, and overall argument strength.

The planned system will process live speech through speech-to-text and an NLP analysis pipeline before presenting feedback through a real-time dashboard.

The current implementation is being developed incrementally, with the machine-learning components evaluated independently before complete real-time integration.


2. Planned System Pipeline

Live Debate
     ↓
Speech Capture
     ↓
Voice Activity Detection
     ↓
Speech-to-Text
     ↓
Speaker Segmentation
     ↓
NLP Analysis
     ↓
Argument Scoring
     ↓
Explainable Feedback
     ↓
Real-Time Dashboard

The final system is planned as a near-real-time analyzer rather than an instantaneous system.


3. Experimental Methodology

The project follows an incremental machine-learning and NLP development workflow.

Dataset preprocessing and baseline models are evaluated first, followed by model comparison and model selection. Validation data is used for model selection where a validation split is available, while held-out test data is reserved for final evaluation.

The main development stages are:

Dataset collection and inspection
Data preprocessing
Leakage checking
Baseline model development
Model comparison
Model selection
Final test evaluation
Argument structure analysis
Bias-aware language analysis
Sentiment analysis
Named Entity Recognition
Evidence extraction
Argument quality analysis
Speech-to-text integration
Real-time backend development
Dashboard development
Complete system integration


4. Dataset: LOGIC Education

The first NLP component developed for BiasLess is logical fallacy detection.

The education portion of the LOGIC dataset was selected for the initial experiment. The dataset contains 13 fallacy categories:

Ad hominem
Ad populum
Appeal to emotion
Circular reasoning
Equivocation
Fallacy of credibility
Fallacy of extension
Fallacy of logic
Fallacy of relevance
False causality
False dilemma
Faulty generalization
Intentional

The original dataset contained:

Split              Samples
Training            1,849
Validation            300
Test                  300
Total               2,449

During preprocessing, duplicate text overlap between the official splits was investigated. Exact duplicate examples occurring across different splits were removed from the later split to prevent data leakage.

After leakage removal:

Split              Samples
Training            1,849
Validation            286
Test                  286
Total               2,421

The final cleaned dataset contains 2,421 examples across 13 fallacy categories.

The cleaned dataset was stored as:

datasets/
└── processed/
    └── logic_edu_clean.csv


5. LOGIC Data Preprocessing

The following preprocessing steps were applied:

Selected the source_article text column.
Selected the updated_label target column.
Removed unnecessary dataset columns.
Normalized whitespace in the text.
Removed leading and trailing whitespace.
Investigated duplicate text entries.
Removed duplicate examples occurring across different dataset splits to prevent leakage.
Preserved the original LOGIC fallacy labels.
Maintained separate TRAIN, DEV, and TEST partitions.

The final leakage check produced:

TRAIN ∩ DEV:   0
TRAIN ∩ TEST:  0
DEV ∩ TEST:    0

Therefore, no exact text appears across the final training, validation, and test sets.


6. Baseline Fallacy Detection Model

6.1 TF-IDF + Logistic Regression

The first baseline model used:

Input Text
    ↓
TF-IDF Vectorization
    ↓
Logistic Regression
    ↓
13 Fallacy Classes

The model used word-level TF-IDF features with unigram and bigram representations and balanced class weights.

The model was trained on the 1,849-example training set and evaluated on the 286-example validation set.

Validation Results

Metric                 Score
Accuracy               44.06%
Macro Precision        40.67%
Macro Recall           42.32%
Macro F1               40.14%
Weighted F1            45.43%

This experiment established the first reproducible baseline for the BiasLess fallacy-detection component.


7. Improved Linear SVM Model

A second approach was evaluated using Linear Support Vector Machine with combined TF-IDF features.

The improved representation combined two types of TF-IDF features:

Word TF-IDF ─────┐
                  ├──→ Linear SVM → Fallacy Class
Character TF-IDF ┘

Word-level features:

Unigram and bigram representation
ngram_range = (1,2)
min_df = 2
max_df = 0.95
Sublinear TF enabled
Up to 50,000 features

Character-level features:

Character n-grams from 3 to 5 characters
min_df = 2
max_df = 0.95
Sublinear TF enabled
Up to 30,000 features

The Linear SVM used:

C = 1.0
class_weight = balanced

The combination of word and character features was evaluated against the Logistic Regression baseline.


8. LOGIC Model Comparison

The validation experiments produced the following results:

Model                                      Accuracy    Macro F1    Weighted F1
TF-IDF + Logistic Regression               44.06%      40.14%      45.43%
Word + Character TF-IDF + Linear SVM       56.64%      51.74%      56.89%

Improvement:

Accuracy: +12.58 percentage points
Macro F1: +11.60 percentage points
Weighted F1: +11.46 percentage points

The Linear SVM with combined word and character TF-IDF features was selected for final evaluation.


