Implementation module:
C:\Users\Rasika\ReasonIQ\backend\speech\transcriber.py

Empirical CPU Benchmarks on Target Laptop:
To choose the most practical model for real-time live debate, benchmarks were performed on recorded debate test clips using CPU with INT8:

Model: base
Load Time: 1.11s
Transcribe Latency: 1.63s
Notes: Multilingual model; higher latency and occasional phonetic drift on accented debate terms.

Model: base.en
Load Time: 0.83s
Transcribe Latency: 0.55s
Notes: Dedicated English model; approximately 3x faster inference; high accuracy on English argument terminology. Selected as the primary default.

Model: small.en
Load Time: 1.70s
Transcribe Latency: 3.79s
Notes: Higher parameter capacity; improved fidelity on complex multi-clause sentences, but higher latency on CPU. Retained as a configurable option for higher-power machines.

The component produces:
- Full transcript text
- Timestamped speech segments
- Transcription confidence scores
- Word-level metadata


33.2 Voice Activity Detection (Silero VAD)

Voice Activity Detection is implemented using:

Silero VAD (TorchScript ONNX/JIT model)

Implementation module:
C:\Users\Rasika\ReasonIQ\backend\speech\vad.py

Configuration Parameters:
- Sampling rate: 16,000 Hz
- Detection threshold: 0.5 (speech probability)
- Minimum speech duration: 250 ms
- Silence padding: 200 ms (preserves start and end phonemes)

The VAD component filters background room noise, microphone clicks, and pauses, preventing empty audio chunks from loading the transcription engine.


33.3 Audio Decoding and PyAV Normalization

Browser microphones stream audio chunks using the MediaRecorder API formatted as WebM/Opus. The backend processes these streaming audio blobs into normalized PCM using:

PyAV (FFmpeg bindings)

Implementation module:
C:\Users\Rasika\ReasonIQ\backend\speech\pipeline.py

Pipeline steps:
1. Decode WebM / Opus / WAV / OGG container formats via PyAV container streams.
2. Resample all multi-channel audio (e.g. 44.1 kHz or 48 kHz stereo) to single-channel 16 kHz Mono float32 PCM.
3. Apply audio amplitude normalization and anti-clipping:
   - Peak amplitude normalized within [-1.0, 1.0].
   - Avoid double-clipping across pipeline stages.
4. Pass contiguous float32 numpy arrays directly to Silero VAD and Whisper without intermediate disk writes.


33.4 ASR Corruption and Speech Quality Gating

A dedicated speech-quality gating layer was built to separate audio capture errors from substantive debate arguments:

Implementation module:
C:\Users\Rasika\ReasonIQ\backend\nlp\content_gate.py

Key Capabilities:
1. Speech Gating:
   - Verifies minimum word count, character count, and speech confidence.
   - Detects severe repetition loops (e.g., hallucinated Whisper loops on trailing silence) and classifies them as corrupted.
2. Distinguishing Acronyms and Valid Short Positions:
   - Normalizes periods in common abbreviations (e.g., "A.I." -> "AI") to prevent false fragment flags.
   - Accurately classifies short substantive claims (e.g., "I agree A.I. should support even creativity not replace it") as valid debate claims.
3. Classifying Defiance and Non-Substantive Retorts:
   - Statements such as "You don't tell me what to do. I will do what I want" are classified as insufficient arguments rather than unintelligible speech.
4. Graceful Downstream Protection:
   - If audio quality is unreliable or corrupted, ReasonIQ displays a transcription warning and suppresses confident fallacy or penalty deductions.


33.5 Multi-Participant Audio Streaming & Utterance Turn Buffering

The system supports distinct audio streams for multiple debate participants:

Implementation module:
C:\Users\Rasika\ReasonIQ\backend\services\audio_service.py

- Independent microphone buffers for Speaker A and Speaker B.
- Turn buffering detects end-of-turn pauses (VAD silence > 800ms) before committing an utterance.
- Previous speaker statements are preserved and passed to the NLP engine as conversational context (context_prev_text).


