35. End-to-End System Integration and Implementation

All formerly planned development stages are now fully implemented and functioning in the active codebase:

35.1 End-to-End Speech-to-NLP Orchestration
The live audio pipeline continuously ingests microphone chunks, verifies speech activity through Silero VAD, transcribes completed utterances via faster-whisper, validates audio clarity via content_gate.py, and routes valid text through the NLP analysis engine.

35.2 Scikit-Learn 1.5.2 Model Retraining
All custom-trained models were retrained natively in the supported Python 3.11 / scikit-learn 1.5.2 environment:
- AAEC Model: models/aaec_tfidf_svm_improved.pkl (Accuracy: 65.43%)
- LOGIC Model: models/logic_tfidf_svm_improved.pkl (Val Accuracy: 56.64%)
- WNC Model: models/wnc_tfidf_svm_improved.pkl (Val Accuracy: 69.47%, Test Accuracy: 67.85%)
All InconsistentVersionWarning warnings were eliminated.

35.3 Context-Aware Logical Fallacy Detection
The system detects 11 formal and informal reasoning patterns:
1. False cause (post-hoc causal leaps)
2. Appeal to ignorance (claiming truth because lack of contrary proof)
3. Hasty generalization (broad claims from singular or anecdotal examples)
4. Slippery slope (extreme chains of unevidenced negative consequences)
5. Ad hominem (personal attacks rather than substantive engagement)
6. Bandwagon / ad populum (claiming correctness based on popularity)
7. Circular reasoning (tautological restatements as proof)
8. Straw man (distorting an opponent's qualified position into an extreme stance)
9. False equivalence (treating fundamentally distinct concepts as identical)
10. False dilemma (artificially limiting choices to two extremes)
11. Appeal to authority (asserting truth solely because an authority agrees)

35.4 Dialectical Straw Man Verification
Straw Man detection compares the current speaker's statement against the preceding speaker's statement (context_prev_text). If Speaker A establishes a qualified position ("AI should be limited in some classroom assessments") and Speaker B asserts a universal caricature ("You want to ban all technology in every school"), ReasonIQ triggers a high-confidence Straw Man detection (confidence: 0.85). If isolated without preceding context, it cautiously returns a lower-confidence signal.

35.5 Authoritative Scoring Engine & Penalty Audit Trail
The scoring system computes an objective composite score (0–100):
- Base Score: Dynamically computed from argument structure (claim vs premise), evidence presence score, and lexical specificity (never defaulted to 65).
- Deductions: Explicitly cataloged into categories:
  * Conduct Interventions (e.g. personal attack, extreme interruption): -8 to -15 pts
  * Logical Fallacy Deductions: -10 to -20 pts based on fallacy severity and confidence
  * Bias-Associated Language Penalties: -5 to -10 pts
- Zero Double-Counting: Fallacy deductions and intervention penalties are tracked independently and never deducted twice for the same event.
- Negative sentiment alone produces zero point deductions.
- All live cards, WebSocket broadcasts, and post-debate reports share the exact same authoritative scores and deduction lists.

35.6 Multi-Client Live Room Management
The FastAPI backend manages persistent debate rooms supporting:
- Debater A (Role: speaker_a, independent audio stream)
- Debater B (Role: speaker_b, independent audio stream)
- Spectator / Judge (Role: spectator, real-time read-only feed)
- Dynamic debate settings updateable in real time (e.g., intervention thresholds, strictness levels) without dropping active participants.

35.7 React Live Dashboard & Real-Time Visualization
The frontend (React + Vite) provides a modern, responsive user experience:
- Live audio recording with interactive waveform visualizers.
- Real-time debater cards displaying transcript, argument components, fallacy badges, evidence meters, and coaching counterarguments.
- Live facilitator intervention banner for severe conduct breaches.
- Authoritative live scores and score progression graphs.

35.8 Post-Debate Comprehensive Reporting
When a debate concludes, the backend generates an authoritative post-debate report:
- Participant head-to-head comparison (Final scores, total claims, premises, fallacies, evidence points).
- Complete chronologically ordered transcript with per-turn analysis.
- Itemized deduction breakdown explaining every penalty applied.
- Export capabilities: Structured JSON and styled printable HTML/PDF.


